"Jev doesn't help: adding the Jev filter leaves line-F1 flat on both lanes, and every F1 change has a confidence interval that includes zero. The cached answers cover all 57 scans in both variants (normal and CAD-free). The A/B run cost about $0.03 and had no API errors.\n\n**Why it can't help much:** a filter can only remove predicted lines, so recall can never go up. The best-F1 lane never proposes 192 of the 307 gold lines. Even a perfect filter would only reach F1 0.545, so the 0.80 target has to come from the engine finding more bends.\n\nHeadline (micro P / R / F1; family-macro F1; \u0394 = change vs the lane alone, 95% CI from a family-level bootstrap):\n\n| lane | arm | keep threshold | all 57 scans: P / R / F1 | all: family-macro F1 | non-calib families (37 scans / 27 families): F1 | non-calib: family-macro F1 | Jev AUC, all | Jev AUC, non-calib |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| best_f1 | (a) lane alone | \u2013 | 0.542 / 0.375 / **0.443** | 0.414 | **0.443** | 0.354 | \u2013 | \u2013 |\n| best_f1 | (b) + Jev filter | 0.35 | 0.624 / 0.345 / **0.444** (\u0394 [-0.016, +0.017]) | 0.381 (\u0394 [-0.090, +0.004]) | **0.444** (\u0394 [-0.028, +0.021]) | 0.308 | 0.694 [0.619, 0.769] | 0.709 [0.611, 0.809] |\n| best_f1 | (c) + Jev CAD-free | 0.61 | 0.620 / 0.345 / **0.444** (\u0394 [-0.024, +0.028]) | 0.425 | **0.421** (\u0394 [-0.051, +0.013]) | 0.350 | 0.721 [0.649, 0.814] | 0.675 [0.583, 0.787] |\n| runtime | (a) lane alone | \u2013 | 0.328 / 0.068 / **0.113** | 0.119 | **0.118** | 0.123 | \u2013 | \u2013 |\n| runtime | (b) + Jev filter | 0.57 | 0.400 / 0.065 / **0.112** | 0.123 | **0.116** | 0.128 | 0.709 [0.555, 0.864] | 0.797 [0.678, 0.973] |\n| runtime | (c) + Jev CAD-free | 0.57 | 0.392 / 0.065 / **0.112** | 0.123 | **0.115** | 0.128 | 0.743 [0.581, 0.881] | 0.804 [0.622, 0.944] |\n\n- **What the filter does on the best-F1 lane:** it removes 37 of 102 false lines and 9 of 115 true lines. Precision rises about 8 points, recall drops about 3, and F1 stays the same. On the zero-bend control scans it cuts false lines from 5 to 1.\n- **Jev at its default 0.5 cut-off makes things worse** on the best-F1 lane: F1 0.381 (\u0394 [-0.123, -0.017]) and family-macro 0.305. Its median score on these candidates is only 0.58, so it rejects many true lines.\n- **Jev adds nothing beyond the evidence it is given.** One scan measurement alone, the share of slices along the line that show two flanges, ranks lines at AUC 0.739. That beats Jev's 0.69\u20130.72, though I picked it after the fact from 16 measurements, so it is optimistic.\n- **CAD-assisted vs CAD-free:** the two are within noise of each other. Giving Jev the CAD information did not help.\n- **Gold11 (human labels):** 0.458 alone, 0.468 with Jev, 0.441 with Jev CAD-free. All CIs include zero. The Gold11 relabel doesn't exist yet, so only human Gold11 is reported. The doc has a re-score command that needs no new Jev calls.\n\nHow it was run and what to watch:\n- **Calibration:** each lane's keep threshold was chosen on 14 calibration families only and saved before any non-calibration line was sent to Jev. The 28 other families were scored afterwards. All scans are already-used development scans, so these numbers are exploratory, not unseen-family accuracy.\n- **Gold has changed since the broad baseline:** part 42288005 was relabelled from 6 to 8 bends, so gold is now 307 lines. That is why the lane alone scores 0.443 instead of the earlier 0.445.\n- **Question wording conflicts with your convention:** the existing Jev question lists \"joggle / offset step\" as a non-bend, but your rule is joggle = 2 bends. Fixing that needs a new question version and a new run; I didn't do it.\n- **Data-retention terms:** the Jev research doc asks for zero-data-retention or enterprise terms with TypeSafe before live calls. I did not check whether those are in place. What was sent carries no coordinates, part IDs or filenames.\n- **Key handling:** the TypeSafe key was loaded from `.env.local` into the calling process only and was never printed or written anywhere.\n\n**New engine results:** this step only covers the Jev A/B; I can't say when the new production engine's results will land. Once that engine writes one prediction file per scan, the same A/B re-runs with the command sequence in `JEV_AB.md` (\"Re-run\" section). For the engine's own output, the line to write per bend is the visible bend edge (apex line) in the scan's own coordinates.\n\nI added one new module, `research/jev/broad_ab_v1.py`, which exports the candidates, picks the thresholds and scores the result. The existing 208 Jev tests still pass, and nothing in the engine was touched.\n\nFiles are in `/Users/idantal/ShermanQC/FullProject/docs/production/runlog/jev_ab/`:\n- JEV_AB.md\n- results.json\n- operating_points.json\n- export/\n- answers/\n- dry_run/\n- features_cache/"